<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahiram sarcophagus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ahirom sarcophagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> sarcophagus is a carved limestone coffin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, generally dated about 1000 BCE, though the date is disputed, and found in 1923 by the French archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> in a shaft tomb of the city's royal necropolis. Its 38-word inscription, in which Ahiram's son records making it for his father and curses anyone who disturbs it, is the oldest known text of any length in the fully developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a>. Its reliefs show a king on a throne of winged sphinxes; it is kept in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahiram-sarcophagus.jpg" alt="Ahiram sarcophagus" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matson Photo Service · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahiram_sarcophag_from_Biblos_XIII-XBC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In late 1923 a landslide in the cliffs around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> exposed a number of Phoenician royal tombs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> excavated the sarcophagus in tomb V of the royal necropolis, ten metres deep. Halfway down the burial shaft a short three-line graffito was found cut into the southern wall. It is usually read as a warning not to go further, though it has recently been proposed that it belongs to an initiation ritual. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> himself is attested in no other ancient Near Eastern source.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Its low-relief panels have been called the major artistic document of the Early Iron Age in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. The main scene shows a king on a throne carved with winged sphinxes, receiving a lotus flower from a priestess. On the lid two male figures face each other with seated lions between them, interpreted by Glenn Markoe as the father and son named in the inscription. The figures, the throne and a table show strong Assyrian influence. Traces of an erased earlier Proto-Byblian inscription are also visible on the monument.</p><p>The inscription runs along parts of the rim and the lid in the Old Phoenician dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. Its formulas were at once recognised as literary, and the sure cutting of the letters suggested to Charles Torrey a script already in common use. The meaning of its last two words is uncertain, but they clearly form a curse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> is not called a king in it. The son's name was long read as Ithobaal I, but a newer reconstruction of the damaged text rejects that reading.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Most scholars place the inscription around 1000 BCE. Others argue for a later date: objects found near the sarcophagus led some to about 850 BCE, and Edith Porada on grounds of art history and Ronald Wallenfels on grounds of the letter forms placed the reliefs in the mid-9th to 8th century BCE, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> finds in the tomb have been taken either as evidence of an early date or of reuse of an older tomb. The sarcophagus, on display in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, heads a list of inscribed objects whose nomination of the Phoenician alphabet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> accepted for its Memory of the World register in 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahiram sarcophagus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ahiram sarcophagus</b>, inscribed by Ahiram’s son Ittobaal and usually dated to around 1000 BCE, though some scholars place it earlier, carries one of the oldest substantial texts in the Phoenician alphabet, and the royal inscriptions of tenth-century Byblos are basic sources for the early alphabet that the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The excavations of Osman Hamdi Bey in the royal necropolis of Sidon in 1887 recovered the sarcophagus of King Tabnit and the Alexander Sarcophagus, and Pierre Montet found the <b>sarcophagus of Ahiram</b> at Byblos in 1923.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
